{"purpose":"Record that the packages carrying the most risk have the least test coverage, and state coverage acceptance criteria tied to risk rather than to a global percentage.","content":"## 1. Observed distribution\nSource-to-test file counts in eka-core are uneven in the direction of risk:\n\n| Package | Source files | Test files | Note |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| metadata | 1 | 1 | identity and repository metadata parsing drives auto-registration |\n| runtime | 20 | 8 | the kernel: authoring, transitions, relations |\n| store | 13 | 2 | one 2,045-line test file covering the canonical store |\n| view | 14 | 15 | the cleanest package, and the best covered |\n| conformance | 15 | 16 | the rule engine, well covered |\n\nTotal test volume is healthy at roughly one test line per source line, so this is a distribution problem, not a volume problem. The two packages that mutate or interpret everything else are the least tested.\n\n## 2. Why this specific shape is dangerous\n`metadata` parses identity from `eka.yaml` and feeds registration; a parsing defect propagates into every subsequent operation while presenting as a clean run. `runtime` owns draft mutation, transitions, and relation rewriting — the only places where authored content can be lost. `store` owns immutability and durability, and its guarantees are the ones the whole model rests on.\n\n## 3. Acceptance criteria\n- `metadata` reaches coverage comparable to `view` or `conformance`, prioritising identity parsing edge cases, malformed input, and namespace reconciliation.\n- `runtime` adds coverage for every path that writes authored content, including failure paths that must leave content intact.\n- `store` coverage explicitly covers crash and concurrent-writer behaviour, extending the existing busy-retry tests to the durability guarantees the model claims.\n- Coverage is reported per package in CI so regressions in the high-risk packages are visible rather than averaged away.\n- The criteria are expressed per package and justified by blast radius, not as a single repository-wide percentage."}